WEBVTT

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:06.560
Hi, this is Dr. Jelena Trajkovic from the CECS Department at Cal State Long Beach.

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:11.280
One more topic that we are going to cover today is topic of truth tables.

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:23.040
We are going to just start here and the next time using examples of properties of Boolean

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:26.640
algebra we are going to continue a little bit deeper in this.

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:29.200
So what are the truth tables?

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:36.960
first of all, there are tables. They are used if you have either an expression or a function

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:42.480
and you want to figure out what is the value of that expression or function. It's not just

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:51.200
that you want to figure out value for one set of inputs, but we want to figure out what are the

00:00:51.200 --> 00:01:00.040
values for expression or a function for all possible inputs. So first of all we

00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:07.500
have to figure out how many different variables or inputs are there either in

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:19.420
expression or function. So let's say that we have n inputs and when each input can

00:01:19.420 --> 00:01:28.380
assume only two possible values, which are 0 and 1, we will have a total of 2 to the

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:56.540
n possible combinations that are going to basically give us all possible input values.

00:01:56.540 --> 00:02:01.300
So let's see that we are going to have an example of a function. I'm not going to list

00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:09.780
it yet. I'm just going to do it halfway. My function is going to be a function of a and b.

00:02:10.980 --> 00:02:20.660
So, total two inputs, input variables. So, I'm going to start making a table. I'm going to write

00:02:20.660 --> 00:02:30.530
a and b. I'm going to make a table for it and on the left hand side of the table are inputs.

00:02:30.530 --> 00:02:42.740
When we have two inputs, we have two to the power of two, four possible combinations.

00:02:42.740 --> 00:02:49.340
For two input variables, it will be, in a little while when you get to the hang of it,

00:02:49.340 --> 00:02:52.700
it will be easy to populate the truth table.

00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:58.620
But when you start having three, four, or five input variables, it is going to be a little

00:02:58.620 --> 00:03:01.660
bit more difficult.

00:03:01.660 --> 00:03:14.680
So in general you have two options, how to populate the table.

00:03:14.680 --> 00:03:24.600
One is to start doing binary counting and in the row 0 you put 0, in row 1 you put binary

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:26.000
1 and so on and so forth.

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:33.160
I mean I'm just giving here example of two variables, sorry, two bits, we can have it

00:03:33.160 --> 00:03:36.340
with three bits.

00:03:36.340 --> 00:03:39.500
So the other way is it's false.

00:03:39.500 --> 00:03:47.180
We start from the right-hand side and we know that we have two to the n, which in our case

00:03:47.180 --> 00:03:51.820
is n is equal to two, so basically two to the two is four.

00:03:51.820 --> 00:03:55.500
We have four possible ways to populate this table.

00:03:55.500 --> 00:04:03.340
So we start from the right-hand side and we write zero, one, zero, one until we do it

00:04:03.340 --> 00:04:06.100
total of four times.

00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:14.600
So this time around for B, for the rightmost variable, the value changes every time that

00:04:14.600 --> 00:04:24.400
you move in the next row. For A, we slow it down. So it's going to be two times we do

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:33.720
zero and then two times we do one. If we were to have another variable of left of

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:43.200
A, what would we do? How many zeros would we have here? And how many ones? And would

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:53.120
we stop only at four or would we need to go to eight total rows? So that is how we

00:04:53.120 --> 00:05:00.280
populate. It is basically a systematic way where the frequency of how often the

00:05:00.280 --> 00:05:08.040
zero changes to one and one to zero is every every time we have change of rows

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:12.880
he's every every second time that we change of row so here is going to be

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:18.640
every fourth time every eighth time and so and so forth. Other thing that you

00:05:18.640 --> 00:05:26.180
need to notice and that is related to the number one option is if you were to

00:05:26.180 --> 00:05:33.360
read this as a binary number and convert it to decimal this one would be in

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:39.520
decimal zero this one will be in decimal one in decimal this is going to be

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:43.040
represent two and this is going to represent three so that's the way to

00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:51.840
to populate the table. So let's go back to the example of a function or equation. We're

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:59.680
going to start with a little bit of a basic one and that's the example that we had previously.

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:12.240
So it's A and B0. So when you are using a third table, you first need to figure out

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:17.200
the values of anything that already has an operator.

00:06:17.200 --> 00:06:21.920
So before we figure out what is a and b naught,

00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:24.320
we have to figure out what is b naught.

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:28.400
So b naught for b naught,

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:34.870
we will look only at vehicle.

00:06:38.410 --> 00:06:43.370
So naught of b when b is equal to zero is one,

00:06:43.370 --> 00:06:50.830
not on B when B is equal to one, is zero, one, and zero.

00:06:51.030 --> 00:06:56.030
Next, we can evaluate entire expression, A and not B.

00:06:56.930 --> 00:07:01.210
For the purpose of this, we are going to look

00:07:01.210 --> 00:07:05.810
into column that depicts values of A

00:07:06.710 --> 00:07:11.710
and column that depicts values of B not.

00:07:11.710 --> 00:07:32.900
and here we are saying 0 and 1 is 0. 0 and 1 is 0. 1 and 1 is 1, bingo. 1 and 0 is 0.

00:07:32.900 --> 00:07:45.630
Now, if I do a little bit of deleting of things and make things a little bit more readable for you,

00:07:45.630 --> 00:08:05.660
you can see that we have defined for all possible values of inputs what are the values for the outputs.

00:08:05.660 --> 00:08:08.660
We are going to practice more of this. Thanks for watching.

